NAME="Kaetzli"
ID=kaetzli
# PRETTY_NAME is what almost everything actually displays, and its absence was visible
# in two places at once: agetty printed the bare fallback "Linux" above the login
# prompt, and fetch fell back to NAME. os-release(5) says consumers should use
# PRETTY_NAME and only fall back to NAME, so shipping just NAME meant relying on the
# fallback everywhere.
PRETTY_NAME="Kaetzli"
HOME_URL="https://github.com/fwilhe2/kaetzli"
ANSI_COLOR="0;36"
# Deliberately no IMAGE_VERSION or BUILD_ID *here*: they are not properties of the
# repository, they are properties of a build, and os-release(5) treats both as optional. An invented value would be worse than an absent one — consumers can tell "not
# versioned" from "version 1", but not from a number that means nothing.
#
# image/build-rootfs.sh appends them when a build actually has them, from KAETZLI_VERSION
# (the snapshot a build of main is released as, docs/proposals/release.md) and
# KAETZLI_BUILD (the commit, which every CI build has). It also folds the version into PRETTY_NAME, which is the
# field the paragraph above says everything displays. A build with neither — a local
# `podman run`, or a PR — produces this file unchanged, which is the honest answer for
# something that is not a release of anything.
#
# What a system or configuration extension built for this image may rely on
# (docs/guides/sysext.md). systemd-sysext merges an extension only if its extension-release
# names ID=kaetzli and the same level, so this number is a promise about the ABI underneath
# an extension — glibc, OpenSSL's major, libseccomp's soname, crun's interface — and
# moves only when that promise breaks. Not VERSION_ID: every release would orphan every
# extension. image/build-extension.sh writes the matching line into what it builds.
SYSEXT_LEVEL=1
CONFEXT_LEVEL=1
